      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Mein, dein, sein...: the possessives",
        "explicacao": "The ending of a possessive agrees with the thing OWNED (gender and number), not with the owner; the owner only chooses the stem (mein, dein, sein, ihr...): «mein Vater», «meine Mutter», «mein Kind», «meine Eltern». The endings are those of «ein»: no ending in the masculine and the neuter, -e in the feminine and the plural. «Sein» means «his» and «ihr» means «her» (and also «their»); «Ihr», with a capital letter, is the formal «your». Careful: «unser» and «euer» lose the -e- before the ending: unsere, eure.",
